 "problems": [
  "Scope: 26 dossiers written against the 8-14 the brief aimed at; ten were added beyond the brief's list because they fill the gaps between listed cultures (houli, beixin, xinglongwa, zhaobaogou, dadiwan, majiayao, hakra-ware, anau, dapenkeng, hoabinhian). All are short (2-9 events).",
  "Twelve cultures run past the 3400 BCE horizon on purpose (yangshao 3000, majiayao 2000, dawenkou 2600, hongshan 2900, qujialing 2500, liangzhu 2300, hakra-ware 3000, dapenkeng 3000, jeulmun 1500, hoabinhian 2000, anau 1100, jomon 300). East Asia, Taiwan and Central Asia between those ends and Cliopatria's first rows (Shang 1600 BCE, Gojoseon 197 BCE, Asuka Japan 540 CE) has no owning group; the follower cultures are listed under counterparts_needing_dossiers.",
  "Brief names Chulmun; the page title and slug are jeulmun-pottery-period (alias Chulmun). Chulmun_pottery_period did not load on the first try; Jeulmun_pottery_period did.",
  "Pages that loaded only after a retry under another title: Jeitun_culture (as Djeitun), Anau_(culture) (as Anau_culture), Hakra_Wares_culture (as Hakra_Ware_culture). Laoguantai_culture and Kuahuqiao_culture did not load at all. Neolithic_China loaded but returned an unusable table and is cited nowhere.",
  "mehrgarh-culture has 4 resolve warnings: the place name Mehrgarh is not in the gazetteer (sa-01's indus-valley-civilization uses it too); the validator treats it as a warning.",
  "Cross-dossier: indus-valley-civilization (sa-01) draws a 50 km Mehrgarh disc from 3300 as its found region and gains the Ghaggar-Hakra country from nobody in 2800; mehrgarh-culture (hands over in 3300) and hakra-ware-culture (ends 3000 into nobody) were written to agree with that. sumerian-city-states and the other xx-01-a notes are untouched.",
  "WebFetch returns a model summary of the page, not the page; the quotation marks in event notes are the quotes as that summary returned them and were not re-checked against the raw page.",
  "Overlaps: no two of the 26 dossiers share a cell in more than one year (checked with the gazetteer); one-year boundary overlaps follow the xx-01-a convention (end year of a culture equals the first year of its successor)."
 ]
